  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Konjunktiv I for earlier events",
     "texto": [
      "To refer to an earlier action, **haben/sein** in Konjunktiv I + Partizip II is used: **Er sagt, er habe den Bericht gelesen** (he says he has read the report). **Die Polizei teilt mit, es seien drei Autos beteiligt gewesen** (the police report that there were three cars involved). The form of the auxiliary and the context place the action in time; there is no single \"past\" in Konjunktiv I."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "When to replace it with Konjunktiv II",
     "texto": [
      "If the Konjunktiv I form coincides with the indicative, Konjunktiv II is usually used so that the reported speech is marked: **Sie sagen, sie kämen morgen** (they say they will come tomorrow). In real use other strategies also appear, such as **würden** + infinitive; the choice depends on the register. Konjunktiv II also expresses hypotheses: interpret it through the context, not as an automatic marker of indirect style."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    }
   ]
  },
